course: dictionary
lesson: 13735
title: Intensifying words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To intensify means to make something stronger, greater or more extreme. When something is intensifying, it is getting stronger right now. «The wind is intensifying, so the waves are getting bigger.» The word «intensifying» tells you the wind is growing stronger, not staying the same.
explain: The word can also describe a small word that makes another word stronger. These are called intensifiers, like «very», «really», «so» and «totally». «The soup was very hot» means hotter than just «hot». The intensifier «very» turns up the strength of «hot».
explain: Be careful, because too many intensifiers make writing weaker, not stronger. «It was really, really, totally, extremely good» sounds empty. One strong, clear word, such as «superb», often does the job better.
fact: To intensify means to make something stronger, greater or more extreme.
fact: «Intensifying» describes something that is growing stronger, like «an intensifying storm».
fact: An intensifier is a word that makes another word stronger, like «very», «really», «so» or «totally».
fact: «The heat is intensifying» means the heat is getting stronger.
fact: Overusing intensifiers can weaken your writing, so pick one strong word when you can.
exercise: «The storm was intensifying all afternoon.» What does intensifying mean here? | Getting weaker | *Getting stronger | Making a loud sound | Going away
exercise: «The soup was very hot.» Which word is the intensifier? | soup | was | *very | The
exercise: «The debate was intensifying as both sides shouted.» What does this tell you? | The argument had finished | *The argument was becoming stronger | Everyone was whispering | Nobody was talking
test: «Pressure is intensifying inside the pipe.» What does intensifying mean? | Breaking into pieces | *Growing stronger | Cooling down | Disappearing
test: Which sentence is clearer and stronger? | *It was superb | It was so very, very good | It was really, really, totally, extremely good
exam: «The heat was intensifying, so the children drank lots of water.» What does intensifying mean? | Stopping suddenly | Turning cold | *Becoming stronger | Becoming weaker
